{
    "solution": "Let $n=2025$.  For each row $i$ let $c_i$ be the column of the unique uncovered unit square in that row; the condition that each column also contains exactly one uncovered square means that $(c_1,\\dots,c_n)$ is a permutation $\\pi$ of $\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$.  The set of covered squares is therefore the set\n\\[\nC=\\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le i,j\\le n,\\;j\\neq \\pi(i)\\}.\n\\]\nA rectangular tile corresponds to a complete bipartite subgraph $R\\times S$ of the bipartite graph with vertex classes $\\{\\text{rows}\\}$ and $\\{\\text{columns}\\}$; the condition that the tile does not contain an uncovered square is equivalent to\n\\[\n\\forall i\\in R\\;\\;\\pi(i)\\notin S.\n\\]\nHence for a tile $R\\times S$ either $S\\subseteq\\{1,\\dots,\\min_{i\\in R}\\pi(i)-1\\}$ (the tile lies completely to the left of the uncovered squares of the rows it uses) or $S\\subseteq\\{\\max_{i\\in R}\\pi(i)+1,\\dots,n\\}$ (the tile lies completely to the right).  Consequently every tile is of one of two types:\n* **left\u2011type** \u2013 it covers only squares that are left of the uncovered square in each of its rows;\n* **right\u2011type** \u2013 it covers only squares that are right of the uncovered square in each of its rows.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lower bound\nOrder the rows so that $\\pi(r_1)<\\pi(r_2)<\\dots<\\pi(r_n)$.  The left\u2011type tiles can only use columns $\\le \\pi(r_{k})-1$ when they contain row $r_{k+1}$.  In particular, the column $\\pi(r_{k})$ (which belongs to the left part of row $r_{k+1}$) cannot be covered by any left\u2011type tile that also contains row $r_{k}$, because $\\pi(r_{k})$ is the uncovered column of $r_{k}$.  Hence each of the $n-1$ columns $\\pi(r_1),\\dots,\\pi(r_{n-1})$ requires a distinct left\u2011type tile.  Therefore at least $n-1$ left\u2011type tiles are necessary.\nThe same argument applied to the right side (ordering the rows by decreasing $\\pi$) shows that at least $n-1$ right\u2011type tiles are needed.\nConsequently any tiling of $C$ must use at least\n\\[\n(n-1)+(n-1)=2n-2\n\\]\ntiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction attaining the bound\nChoose any permutation; for concreteness take the cyclic shift\n\\[\n\\pi(i)=\\begin{cases}i+1,&1\\le i<n,\\\\[2pt]1,&i=n.\\end{cases}\n\\]\nFor each column $j$ let $r=\\pi^{-1}(j)$ be the row of the uncovered square in that column.\n* If $r>1$, place a vertical $ (r-1)\\times1$ tile covering the squares $(1,j),(2,j),\\dots,(r-1,j)$ \u2013 the part of column $j$ that lies **above** the uncovered square.\n* If $r<n$, place a vertical $(n-r)\\times1$ tile covering the squares $(r+1,j),\\dots,(n,j)$ \u2013 the part of column $j$ that lies **below** the uncovered square.\nThese tiles are disjoint, they cover every square of $C$, and no tile contains an uncovered square.  For the column that contains the uncovered square in the first row the \u201cabove\u2019\u2019 tile is empty, and for the column that contains the uncovered square in the last row the \u201cbelow\u2019\u2019 tile is empty.  Hence the total number of tiles is\n\\[\n\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{non\u2011empty above tiles}}+\\underbrace{(n-1)}_{\\text{non\u2011empty below tiles}}=2n-2.\n\\]\nThus the bound $2n-2$ is attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Conclusion\nBoth the lower\u2011bound argument and the explicit construction show that the minimum possible number of tiles is $2n-2$.  For $n=2025$ this number equals\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2=4048.\n\\]\nTherefore Matilda needs at least, and can achieve, **4048** tiles.\n",
