s are all less than the smallest uncovered column among rows in the tile. So we could potentially cover many columns with a single left-type tile, reducing the number of left-type tiles needed. The solution's lower bound argument seems to assume that each column pi(r_k) requires a distinct left-type tile, but that may be false because we could have a left-type tile covering multiple columns pi(r_k) for different k, as long as the rows included in the tile have uncovered columns larger than all those columns. For instance, consider rows r_{k+1},...,r_n (with uncovered columns > pi(r_k)). The left-type tile could have columns S = {pi(r_1),...,pi(r_k)} (i.e., all columns up to pi(r_k)). Then for each row i in R (i.e., rows r_{k+1}...r_n), pi(i) > pi(r_k) >= any column in S, so S is left of pi(i). So that tile would be left-type and would cover all squares in columns pi(r_1) through pi(r_k) for rows r_{k+1}...r_n. That would cover many columns with a single tile. However, we must also cover squares in those columns for rows r_1...r_k (except the uncovered squares). For rows r_1...r_k, the column pi(r_j) is the uncovered column for row r_j, so we cannot cover that column for row r_j. But we need to cover column pi(r_j) for rows r_{j+1}...r_n (which we already cover with the left-type tile). For rows r_1...r_{j-1}, column pi(r_j) is to the right of their uncovered column (since pi(r_{j-1}) < pi(r_j)), so those squares could be covered by a right-type tile. So each column pi(r_j) could be covered by a left-type tile covering rows with larger uncovered columns, and a right-type tile covering rows with smaller uncovered columns. So each column might require at least two tiles (one left-type, one right-type), except the extreme columns which require only one type. So total number of tiles needed is at least 2*(n-2) + 2 = 2n-2? Let's compute: For each column j (except the smallest and largest), we need at least one left-type tile and at least one right-type tile that include column j. However, a single left-type tile can cover multiple columns simultaneously, as long as the rows included are those with uncovered columns larger than all those columns. Similarly, a single right-type tile can cover multiple columns simultaneously, as long as the rows included have uncovered columns smaller than all those columns. So we could possibly cover all columns with a small number of left-type tiles and a small number of right-type tiles. For example, we could have one left-type tile covering all rows except the row with the smallest uncovered column, and columns all except the smallest uncovered column? Let's think: Suppose we take left-type tile with rows R = {r_2,...,r_n} (all rows except r_1) and columns S = {1,..., pi(r_1)-1} (i.e., all columns left of the smallest uncovered column). That tile would cover all squares in those rows and columns left of pi(r_1). However, columns left of pi(r_1) are all columns less than the smallest uncovered column. But there may be many columns left of pi(r_1) that are not uncovered squares (i.e., they are not pi(i) for any i). Actually pi is a permutation of {1,...,n}, so each column is an uncovered column for exactly one row. So the columns left of pi(r_1) are those columns that are not uncovered squares for any row? Wait, pi(r_1) is the smallest uncovered column. So columns 1,..., pi(r_1)-1 are not uncovered columns for any row, because pi(r_1) is the smallest uncovered column. So those columns are not uncovered squares at all. So they need to be covered entirely (all rows). So we could cover them with a left-type tile covering all rows (including r_1) and columns 1,..., pi(r_1)-1. But can we include row r_1? For row r_1, pi(r_1) is the uncovered column, which is > any column in S (since S is columns less than pi(r_1)). So S is left of pi(r_1), so left-type tile can include row r_1 as well. So we could have a left-type tile covering all rows and columns left of pi(r_1). That would cover all squares in those columns. That is a single left-type tile covering many columns. Similarly, we could have a right-type tile covering all rows and columns right of pi(r_n) (the largest uncovered column). But there are no columns right of pi(r_n) because pi(r_n) is the largest uncovered column, so that set is empty. So we need to cover columns between uncovered columns. For each interval between consecutive uncovered columns, we could potentially cover those columns with a left-type tile covering rows with uncovered columns larger than the interval, or a right-type tile covering rows with uncovered columns smaller than the interval. But perhaps we can cover all columns between pi(r_k) and pi(r_{k+1}) with a single tile? Let's examine.\n\nWe have uncovered columns at positions pi(r_1) < pi(r_2) < ... < pi(r_n). The columns that are not uncovered are all other columns. But since pi is a permutation, every column is uncovered for exactly one row. So there are no columns that are not uncovered. Wait, each column is exactly one uncovered square. So each column j is uncovered at row pi^{-1}(j). So there are no columns that are not uncovered. So each column j has exactly one uncovered square at row r_j = pi^{-1}(j). So we need to cover all squares in column j except that one. So for each column j, we need to cover squares in rows i ≠ r_j. So each column j must be covered for all rows except one. So we cannot have a column that is not uncovered. So the earlier thought about columns left of pi(r_1) being not uncovered is wrong: pi(r_1) is the smallest uncovered column, but there are columns less than pi(r_1) that are also uncovered for some rows? Wait, pi is a permutation of {1,...,n}. So pi(r_1) is the smallest value among pi(i). So there cannot be any column less than pi(r_1) that is uncovered, because pi(r_1) is the smallest uncovered column. But pi is a permutation, meaning each column appears exactly once as pi(i). So if pi(r_1) is the smallest value, then columns 1,..., pi(r_1)-1 are not in the image of pi, i.e., they are not uncovered squares. But that contradicts the definition that each column must have exactly one uncovered square. Wait, the problem statement says each column must have exactly one uncovered unit square. That means for each column j, there is exactly one row i such that (i,j) is uncovered. So the set of uncovered squares is a set of n squares, one per row and one per column, i.e., a permutation. So pi is a permutation of {1,...,n}, meaning pi(i) is the column of the uncovered square in row i. Since pi is a permutation, its image is the entire set {1,...,n}. So pi(r_1) is the smallest value among {1,...,n}, which is 1. So pi(r_1) = 1. Similarly, pi(r_n) = n. So the ordering of rows by pi yields pi(r_1) = 1, pi(r_n) = n. So the columns are exactly the numbers 1,...,n, each appearing exactly once as pi(i). So the columns are exactly the uncovered columns. So there are no columns that are not uncovered. So the earlier thought about columns left of pi(r_1) being not uncovered is false because pi(r_1) = 1, so there are no columns less than 1. Similarly, pi(r_n) = n, so there are no columns greater than n. So the ordering yields pi(r_k) = k (if we reorder rows accordingly). Actually, we can reorder rows such that pi(r_k) = k. Since pi is a permutation, we can rename rows so that pi(i) = i for i=1,...,n. So the uncovered squares are at positions (i,i). So the problem reduces to covering all squares except the main diagonal. So the solution's lower bound argument can be considered for the identity permutation.\n\nThus the lower bound argument: For identity permutation, left-type tiles are those that cover only squares left of the diagonal in each row they include (i.e., columns < row index). Right-type tiles cover only squares right of the diagonal (columns > row index). So each left-type tile can only cover squares in rows i and columns j with j < i (i.e., strictly below the diagonal). Similarly, right-type tiles cover squares above the d