st have rows > d. So the row intervals for tiles covering left side cells in column c are subsets of {c+1,...,n}, and for column d are subsets of {d+1,...,n}. Since these sets are nested (the set for column d is a subset of the set for column c if d>c), is it possible for a tile to cover cells from both columns c and d? Let's think: Suppose we have a tile that covers cells in column c and column d, both left side cells. Then the tile's row interval must be a subset of both {c+1,...,n} and {d+1,...,n}. Since d > c, {d+1,...,n} is a subset of {c+1,...,n}. So the row interval must be a subset of {d+1,...,n}. So it's possible for a tile to cover cells from both columns c and d as long as its rows are > d. But the tile also must include column c and column d. Since the tile is a rectangle, its column interval includes all columns between c and d, inclusive. So the tile would also cover cells in columns between c and d, including column c+1,...,d-1. For those columns, the tile would cover some cells left of diagonal? Let's examine: For column e with c < e < d, the tile's rows are > d > e, so rows > e, which are left side cells for column e. So the tile would also cover left side cells in those intermediate columns. So a tile covering left side cells in multiple columns would be a rectangle spanning a range of columns and rows > the maximum column index among them. This seems possible. Let's test with small n: n=3. Left side cells are (2,1), (3,1), (3,2). Could we cover all three left side cells with a single rectangle? The rectangle covering rows 2-3 and columns 1-2 would be a 2x2 rectangle covering cells (2,1), (2,2), (3,1), (3,2). But (2,2) and (3,2) are on the diagonal? Actually (2,2) is uncovered (since diagonal). So that rectangle would cover (2,2), which is not allowed. So we cannot include column 2 because row 2 is included (since rectangle includes rows 2-3). But we could consider rectangle covering rows 3-3 (just row 3) and columns 1-2: that would cover (3,1) and