les. So still n-1.\n\nThus minimal for left region appears to be n-1. Let's try to prove lower bound: For each column c (1 ≤ c ≤ n-1), the squares in column c that are left of diagonal are rows r > c. So the set of squares in column c is a vertical line segment from row c+1 to n. Any rectangle covering any of these squares cannot include any row ≤ c, as argued. So each rectangle covering column c must have its rows > c. Therefore, for column c, the rectangle's rows must be a subset of {c+1,...,n}. For column c+1, the rectangle's rows must be a subset of {c+2,...,n}. So any rectangle that covers squares in both column c and column c+1 must have rows > c+1. So it's possible. So the argument that each column requires a distinct tile is not correct. However, we can consider the \"lowest row\" of each rectangle covering left side cells. Suppose we have a rectangle covering left side cells in some set of columns. Let the smallest column index among those columns be c_min. Then the rectangle's rows must be > c_min. So the rectangle cannot cover any square in column c_min that is at row c_min+1? Actually it can cover squares in column c_min with rows > c_min. So it can cover all squares in column c_min except maybe some at the top? Let's think: The rectangle's row interval is some [r_low, r_high] with r_low > c_min. So it covers squares (r,c) for c in some range [c_min, c_max] and rows r in [r_low, r_high]. So for column c_min, it covers squares from rows r_low..r_high, which are a subset of rows > c_min. So it does not cover squares in column c_min that are rows between c_min+1 and r_low-1 (if any). So there may be uncovered squares in column c_min that are not covered by this rectangle. So you might need additional rectangles to cover those. So the minimal number of rectangles might be at least n-1, but we need to prove that.\n\nWe can think of the left region as a Young diagram shape. The minimal number of rectangles needed to partition a Young diagram into rectangles is