    "solution": "### Summary ###\n\n**a. Verdict:** I have successfully solved the problem. The final answer is 4048.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**\n- **Construction (upper bound):** Place the uncovered unit squares on the main diagonal, i.e., at positions $(i,i)$ for $i=1,2,\\dots,2025$.  The cells to the left of the diagonal form a lower\u2011triangular region and the cells to the right form an upper\u2011triangular region.  Tile each column $j$ of the lower region with a vertical $1\\times (2025-j)$ rectangle (rows $j+1$ through $2025$, column $j$) and each column $j$ of the upper region with a vertical $1\\times (j-1)$ rectangle (rows $1$ through $j-1$, column $j$).  This uses $(2025-1)+(2025-1)=4048$ tiles.\n- **Lower bound:**  For any admissible placement of the $2025$ uncovered squares, consider the unique row $r_{c}$ whose uncovered square lies in column $c+1$ (for $c=1,\\dots,2024$).  The cell $(r_{c},c)$ is the rightmost cell of the left side of row $r_{c}$.  Any rectangle covering this cell must lie completely to the left of the uncovered square in that row, hence its rightmost column is exactly $c$.  Consequently each $c$ forces a distinct \"left\u2011side\" rectangle; at least $2024$ such rectangles are necessary.  An analogous argument applied to the right side (using the cells $(r'_{c},c)$ with $c=2,\\dots,2025$) shows that at least another $2024$ \"right\u2011side\" rectangles are required.  Therefore any tiling needs at least $2\\cdot2024=4048$ tiles.\n\nCombining the construction and the lower bound yields the exact minimum.\n\n### Detailed Solution ###\n\n**Problem restatement.**\nWe have a $N\\times N$ board with $N=2025$.  We must place axis\u2011parallel rectangular tiles whose sides lie on grid lines, no two tiles may overlap, and each unit square is covered by at most one tile.  Exactly one unit square in every row and in every column must remain uncovered.  The task is to find the smallest possible number of tiles.\n\n**Notation.**\nLet $N$ denote the board size (later we substitute $N=2025$).  Let $f:\\{1,\\dots,N\\}\\to\\{1,\\dots,N\\}$ be the permutation defined by the uncovered squares: the uncovered square in row $i$ is at column $f(i)$.  For each row $i$ define\n\\[\nL_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j < f(i)\\},\\qquad R_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid f(i)< j\\le N\\}.\n\\]\nThus $L_i$ is the set of cells to the left of the hole in row $i$, and $R_i$ the set to the right.  By the condition on uncovered squares we have $|L_i|+|R_i|=N-1$ for every $i$.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**1. Upper bound \u2013 an explicit tiling with $2N-2$ tiles.**\nPlace the uncovered squares on the main diagonal, i.e. $f(i)=i$ for all $i$.  Then\n\\[\nL_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid 1\\le j < i\\},\\qquad R_i = \\{(i,j)\\mid i< j\\le N\\}.\n\\]\nThe cells of $\\bigcup_i L_i$ form the lower\u2011triangular region $\\{(i,j)\\mid j<i\\}$.  For each column $c$ with $1\\le c\\le N-1$ consider the vertical rectangle\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{left}} = \\{(i,c)\\mid c+1\\le i\\le N\\}.\n\\]\nAll cells of column $c$ that belong to the lower\u2011triangular region are exactly those rows $i>c$, so $T_c^{\\text{left}}$ covers them and contains no uncovered square.  The $N-1$ rectangles $T_c^{\\text{left}}$ (for $c=1,\\dots,N-1$) partition the whole lower\u2011triangular region.\n\nSimilarly the cells of $\\bigcup_i R_i$ form the upper\u2011triangular region $\\{(i,j)\\mid i<j\\}$.  For each column $c$ with $2\\le c\\le N$ define\n\\[\nT_c^{\\text{right}} = \\{(i,c)\\mid 1\\le i\\le c-1\\}.\n\\]\nAgain these $N-1$ rectangles are disjoint, avoid all uncovered squares, and together cover the entire upper\u2011triangular region.\n\nThus the whole board except the $N$ diagonal holes is tiled by\n\\[(N-1)+(N-1)=2N-2\\]\nrectangles.  For $N=2025$ this gives a tiling with $2\\cdot2025-2=4048$ tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**2. Lower bound \u2013 at least $2N-2$ tiles are necessary.**\nWe work with an arbitrary permutation $f$ (i.e. with an arbitrary admissible placement of the $N$ uncovered squares).\n\n*Lemma 1 (rightmost cells of left intervals).*\nFor each $c\\in\\{1,\\dots,N-1\\}$ let $r_c$ be the unique row with $f(r_c)=c+1$ (such a row exists because $f$ is a permutation).  The cell $(r_c,c)$ belongs to $L_{r_c}$ and is the **rightmost** cell of that left interval.\n\n*Proof.*  By definition $L_{r_c}=\\{(r_c,j)\\mid j<c+1\\}$, so $(r_c,c)\\in L_{r_c}$.  No cell of $L_{r_c}$ has column larger than $c$, hence $(r_c,c)$ is the rightmost one.  \u220e\n\n*Lemma 2 (a left\u2011side rectangle is forced to end at a prescribed column).*\nLet $R$ be any rectangle that covers the cell $(r_c,c)$.  Because $R$ cannot contain the uncovered square $(r_c,c+1)$, the whole rectangle must lie strictly to the left of column $c+1$ in row $r_c$.  Consequently the rightmost column of $R$ is exactly $c$.\n\n*Proof.*  If the rightmost column of $R$ were $<c$, the cell $(r_c,c)$ would not be covered.  If it were $>c$, then column $c+1$ would also belong to $R$, contradicting the fact that $(r_c,c+1)$ is uncovered.  Hence the rightmost column equals $c$. \u220e\n\n*Corollary 1.*  For each $c\\in\\{1,\\dots,N-1\\}$ there must exist a rectangle whose rightmost column is $c$ and which lies entirely to the left of the uncovered squares of all rows it touches.  Such a rectangle is called a **left\u2011side rectangle**.\n\nSince a rectangle has a single rightmost column, a single left\u2011side rectangle can serve at most one value of $c$.  Therefore we need at least $N-1$ distinct left\u2011side rectangles.\n\nThe symmetric argument applied to the right side yields the following.\n\n*Lemma 3 (leftmost cells of right intervals).*\nFor each $c\\in\\{2,\\dots,N\\}$ let $s_c$ be the unique row with $f(s_c)=c-1$.  The cell $(s_c,c)$ is the **leftmost** cell of $R_{s_c}$.\n\n*Lemma 4 (a right\u2011side rectangle is forced to start at a prescribed column).*\nAny rectangle covering $(s_c,c)$ must have its leftmost column equal to $c$; otherwise it would either miss $(s_c,c)$ or would contain the uncovered square $(s_c,c-1)$.\n\nConsequently we need at least one distinct **right\u2011side rectangle** for each $c=2,\\dots,N$, i.e. another $N-1$ rectangles.\n\n*Conclusion of the lower bound.*\nAny tiling must contain at least $N-1$ left\u2011side rectangles and at least $N-1$ right\u2011side rectangles, altogether at least $2N-2$ tiles.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**3. Minimality.**\nThe construction of Section\u202f1 uses exactly $2N-2$ tiles, and Section\u202f2 shows that no tiling can use fewer.  Hence the minimum possible number of tiles is\n\\[\n2N-2.\n\\]\nFor the concrete board $N=2025$ we obtain\n\\[\n2\\cdot2025-2 = 4048.\n\\]\nThus Matilda needs at least $4048$ tiles, and $4048$ tiles are sufficient.\n\n**Answer:** $4048$.\n",
    "final_answer": 4048
}
